hey don't need it. Unshown W8 has 3 left. \n\nBut I have a Pung of T8, which is a different suit. So discarding W8 might not affect that. \n\nAlternatively, discarding J2. Since it's a Green Dragon, and unshown J2 has 4 left. If others are going for a Pung of Green Dragons, they might need it. But since I have only one, and there are four left, maybe it's safe. \n\nConsidering all this, I think discarding W3 might be a good move. It's part of a chow that the player across already has, so they might not need it. Also, keeping W4 might be useful if I can form a chow with W3 and W5 (but I don't have W5). However, since I can't form that, maybe it's not helpful. \n\nAlternatively, discarding T2. The next player played T2, so maybe they don't need it. Unshown T2 has 3 left. \n\nBut I'm not sure. Let me check the valid actions again. The valid actions are Play W4, W3, W8, W1, T3, T2, B8, W7, W9, J2, B9. \n\nIf I discard W1, which has 4 left, maybe that's the safest. But W1 is a terminal, which others might want. \n\nAlternatively, discard T3. Unshown T3 has 4 left, which is safe. \n\nHmm. I'm a bit stuck. Let me think about the possible patterns again. If I can form a hand with four chows and a pair, that's All Chows (2 points). Let's see. \n\nIf I discard W3, then my hand would be W1, W4, W7, W8, W9, T2, T3, B8, B9, J2. \n\nLooking for chows:\n\n- W7, W8, W9: that's a chow.\n- T2, T3: need another T4 for a chow. But I have T4 in my history? Wait, my hand has T2 and T3. If I discard T3, maybe I can form a chow with T2 and T3 if I have T4. But I don't have T4. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard W4, then I have W3. Maybe with W3 and W4 (but I'm discarding one of them). \n\nThis is getting complicated. Maybe the best is to discard a tile that's not part of any potential chow. For example, J2. Since it's an honor tile, and I have only one, discarding it might not help my hand, but it's an honor tile. \n\nAlternatively, discard B8 or B9. They are terminals. Unshown B8 has 2 left,