their existing Chow. They might have another one. But the unshown has 2 left. \n\nHmm. I'm not sure. Maybe I should go with T2. \n\nWait, let me check the unshown tiles again. T2 has 3 left. If I discard T2, there are still two more. The previous player has played T2, but they might not have all of them. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard W3, there are 2 left. \n\nI think the best move is to discard a tile that's not part of any of my potential melds. Since I have W7, W8, W9, keeping those is important. T2 is a single tile, not part of any meld. So discarding T2 would be the safest. \n\nSo the answer is Play T2.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Discarding T2 is the safest option as it is a single tile not involved in any potential melds and minimizes the risk of aiding opponents.  \nReason: T2 is not part of any current or potential melds in my hand. Opponents' melds do not suggest immediate need for T2, and discarding it avoids exposing tiles critical for future chows like W7-W8-W9 or B3.  \nAnswer: Play T2"}
