ain. If I can form a pair and a pung. For example, if I have two of a tile (pair) and three of another (pung). But I only have one of each tile in my hand. So maybe I need to form a pair and a chow. For example, if I have a pair of W3 and a chow of W3-W4-W5. But I don't have W4 or W5. Alternatively, if I can form a pair of T4 and a chow. But I don't have T3 or T5. Hmm. This is tricky. Maybe the best move is to play a tile that is less likely to be needed by others, so that I can keep the tiles that can help form a chow or pung. For example, playing W1 since there's a pung already, maybe it's safe. Or playing B9 since my chow is already formed. Alternatively, playing T4 since it's a middle tile and might be less likely to be needed. Let me check the unshown tiles. T4 has 1 left. If I play T4, there's a chance someone might need it for a chow. The next player has a chow of B2-B3-B4. If they have a B4, maybe they need a T4 for another chow? Not sure. Alternatively, if I play W3, there are two W3s left. Maybe someone can chi if they have W2 or W4. The previous player has a chow of W7-W8-W9, so they might not need W3. The player across has a pung of W1. So maybe playing W3 is a good option. Alternatively, playing W1 is also possible. Let me think about the possible next moves. If I play W3, and someone chi it, that's a possible action. If I play W1, maybe the player across can chi it since they have a pung of W1. But they already have three, so they might not need it. So maybe playing W3 is better. Alternatively, playing T4. Hmm. I'm a bit stuck. Maybe I should play the tile that is least useful for forming my own hand. Since I need to form a pair and a pung or chow, maybe discarding a tile that's not part of any potential meld. For example, B9 is part of a chow already, so keeping it might help if I can form another chow. T4 is part of a lower suit. W1 is a terminal. W3 is a middle tile. W8 is part of a chow. Maybe playing W8 is a good option. Let me check. If I play W8